Buddy Allocator -- CS 452 HW2
=============================

Files
-----
  balloc.h       the given interface; documented, otherwise unchanged
  balloc.c       the interface: argument checks, rounding, messages
  freelist.h/.c  the Buddy algorithm: free lists and buddy bitmaps
                 (interface changed from the handout; see Design)
  bbm.h/.c       provided: buddy-pair bitmaps, buddy addresses;
                 documented, code unchanged
  bm.h/.c        provided: a general bitmap; documented, code unchanged
  utils.h/.c     mmap() memory, power-of-two arithmetic, bit operations
  error.h        HW1's WARN/ERROR macros; unchanged
  test.h/.c      the test harness, and main()
  *_test.c       each module's tests
  GNUmakefile    the course makefile's rules, inlined so this
                 directory builds on its own; deq/ includes it
  deq/           the HW1 deque and its test suite, unchanged, run on
                 this allocator through the given wrapper.c

Building
--------
This directory is self contained; nothing outside it is needed.

  make           builds ./balloc, the test suite
  make run       builds and runs it
  make valgrind  runs it under valgrind
  make clean     removes the built files

  cd deq; make run   builds ./deq (HW1's deque and tests, with
                     wrapper.c) and runs it; see "The wrapper" below

./balloc and ./deq each exit 0 iff every test passes.

Design
------
The modules form a stack, each using only those below it:

  balloc     public interface: checks arguments, rounds requests to an
             order, reports misuse
  freelist   the Buddy algorithm: split, merge, find a block's order
  bbm        a bitmap indexed by buddy pair, and buddy arithmetic
  bm         a plain bitmap
  utils      mmap(), powers of two, bits

A block of order e is 2^e bytes, l <= e <= u, and starts at a multiple
of 2^e from the pool's base.  Its buddy, the other half of the
order-(e+1) block that contains it, is at an offset differing only in
bit e.

bcreate() rounds size up to a multiple of 2^l, and maps the pool, a
small descriptor, and the free lists' bookkeeping.  No other call maps
memory, and nothing calls malloc(), so the allocator can replace it.
The pool is tiled with the largest aligned blocks of order <= u: blocks
of 2^u, then one block per 1-bit of the remainder, largest first.  So a
pool that is not a power of two, or is larger than 2^u, gets reasonably
sized blocks.

Free blocks are kept on one singly linked list per order.  The link is
the first word of each free block, so 2^l must hold a pointer.
Allocated blocks hold no management data at all.

Each order also has two bitmaps, with a bit per buddy pair:

  free   flipped whenever either buddy enters or leaves the list, so it
         is 1 iff exactly one buddy is on the list.  A block being freed
         is not on it, so the bit says whether its buddy is: whether to
         merge.  (The Linux kernel's scheme.)
  alloc  set when either buddy is allocated at that order, cleared when
         the pair merges.  A block's order is the smallest order whose
         alloc bit covers it: every pair inside an allocated block has
         both bits 0.  That also lets bfree() and bsize() reject a
         pointer that is not the start of an allocated block.

balloc() takes a block of the order a request needs.  If that list is
empty, it takes a larger block, recursively, splits it, keeps the lower
half, and frees the upper.  bfree() finds the order, then merges while
the buddy is free.  A freed block whose buddy is not free leaves the
pair's alloc bit set; if the buddy was split, the bit is stale but
harmless: a size lookup never looks above a block's own order, and the
pair's eventual merge clears it.

The freelist interface differs from the handout's: a FreeList records
its pool's base, l and u, so they are not passed (possibly
inconsistently) to every call.  And freeliststr() renders the pool as a
string, "0:A16 16:F16 32:F32 ...", which the tests compare exactly.

Semantics
---------
  bcreate  a pool of size bytes, blocks of 2^l..2^u bytes; needs
           2^l >= sizeof(void *) and l <= u <= 30
  balloc   a block of the smallest order holding size bytes, at least
           2^l; 0 if size exceeds 2^u or no free block is large enough
  bfree    returns a block; bfree of 0 does nothing
  bsize    the block's size (a power of two), not the request's
  bprint   the block map, each free list, and each bitmap
  bdelete  unmaps the pool; bdelete of 0 does nothing

Errors
------
Bad bcreate() arguments, or a failed mmap(), are reported by WARN(),
and bcreate() returns 0.  Passing bfree() or bsize() anything but an
allocated block of the pool (its middle, a freed block, a block of
another pool, a stack address) is reported, and does nothing (bsize()
returns 0).  So is a zero pool.  Running out of memory, or asking for
more than 2^u bytes, is not an error: balloc() returns 0, as malloc()
does.

Testing
-------
The suite runs 93 tests, bottom up: utils, bm, bbm, freelist, balloc.
The freelist tests check the pool's exact block map after splits,
merges, exhaustion, frees in scrambled order, pools that are not a power
of two (whose last blocks have no buddy), and a pool at an unaligned
address.  The balloc tests cover argument checking, rounding, alignment,
non-overlap, fragmentation, misuse, independent pools, and a stress test
of 50,000 random allocations and frees that checks every block's
contents and ends fully merged.  14 messages on stderr are expected,
each announced: the misuse tests showing the error messages.

The wrapper
-----------
deq/ holds HW1's deq.c, deq.h, error.h and main.c, unchanged, and the
given wrapper.c, which defines malloc(), free() and realloc() on a
4096-byte pool (l=4, u=12).  On Linux those definitions replace libc's,
for libc's own calls too (strdup(), asprintf(), stdio buffers).  The
wrapper needs gcc: it uses a nested function.

stdout's buffer comes from malloc(), sized by where stdout goes:
typically 1024 bytes for a terminal, but 4096 for a file or pipe.  4096
is the whole pool, leaving nothing for the deque.  So run ./deq with
stdout on a terminal (make run), not redirected.

AI Use
------
Claude Code was used to help write and expand the tests, format code, and correct
to correct and expand documentation where needed. I again used it for tutor functionality,
mmap, and the buddy system.
